I deployed a Spring Boot authentication service to a real domain for the first time. It started cleanly, the health endpoint returned `UP`, and the logs were quiet. Then every browser request failed with **403 Invalid CORS request**, the passkey registration reported that the relying party ID was not a registrable suffix of the origin, and the rate limiter turned out to be doing nothing at all.

All three variables were in the environment. I checked. They were spelled correctly, they were in the container, `printenv` showed them.

## Relaxed binding is stricter than it looks

Spring Boot maps environment variables onto properties by a set of rules usually described as forgiving. They are forgiving in one direction only. For a property named:

```
auth-platform.cors.allowed-origins
```

the environment variable is formed by uppercasing and turning **every** dash and dot into an underscore:

```
AUTH_PLATFORM_CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS
```

What I had written was `AUTH_PLATFORM_CORS_ALLOWEDORIGINS` — dashes removed in one segment, converted in another. It is the kind of name that looks right, and it matches nothing.

Here is the part that cost me the afternoon: **a name that matches nothing does not fail.** The property simply keeps whatever default `application.yml` gave it. My default was `http://localhost:3000`, because that is what you develop against. So the service started, reported healthy, and rejected every real request — while presenting as a browser problem.

## The same trap, one level deeper

Later, configuring an SSO client, I wrote this:

```java
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "auth-platform.sso")
public class SsoProperties {
    private Map<String, Client> clients = new LinkedHashMap<>();
}
```

and set `AUTH_PLATFORM_SSO_CLIENTS_PORTFOLIO_SECRET`. Every request came back **unknown client**.

Binding a named property works because the binder already knows the name it is looking for — it can generate the candidate environment variable and check whether it exists. A **map** is the opposite problem: the keys are not known in advance, so the binder has to work backwards from `AUTH_PLATFORM_SSO_CLIENTS_PORTFOLIO_SECRET` to a property path. It cannot. It has no way to tell which underscores were dots and which were dashes, so it cannot know that the key is `portfolio`.

Maps do not bind from environment variables when the prefix contains a dash. They bind from YAML perfectly well, which is exactly why this survives development.

I flattened it:

```java
private String clientId = "portfolio";
private String clientSecret;
private List<String> redirectUris = new ArrayList<>();
```

There was one relying party. The map had been generality I did not need, and it happened to also be unbindable.

## What actually helps

The fix is not to be more careful with names. It is to make a missing value impossible to ignore.

```java
@Validated
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "auth-platform.issuer")
public class JwtIssuerProperties {
    @NotBlank private String issuer;
    @Positive private long accessTokenExpiryMinutes;
}
```

That `@Validated` is load-bearing. Without it, a missing `access-token-expiry-minutes` binds to `0`, and the service returns HTTP 200 with an access token every consumer rejects as already expired — a total outage that presents as a client bug. With it, the application refuses to start and names the property.

For anything that cannot be validated into existence, a startup check that throws beats a default. And where a default is genuinely appropriate, make it the **safe** one: a rate limiter that defaults to off is a rate limiter that will one day be off in production without anybody deciding that.

## The general shape

Every one of these failures had the same form: a value was missing, something reasonable happened instead, and the system reported success. That is worse than crashing. A service that will not start gets fixed in minutes. A service that starts with the wrong configuration gets debugged from the browser, from the proxy, from CORS documentation — anywhere except the one line where the name was wrong.

If a configuration value matters, make its absence fatal.
